{
  "abstract": "Paper 164 shows that a rational Diophantine septuple contains at most six regular quadruples and leaves open whether six can occur. Up to relabelling there are 219 admissible arrangements of six regular quadruples among seven entries. We list them with their automorphism groups, signed ranks and covering data, and decide four that were open: orbit 10, orbit 40, 656451 and 120833. For each of the four, no seven distinct nonzero rationals satisfy the six regularity equations. The 21 square conditions are not used, and the 21 values 1 + x_i x_j may vanish. Orbit 10 has unit ideal after saturation by the coordinates and differences: an explicit product of six linear forms lies in the ideal over Q, and none of its 62 proper subproducts does. Orbit 40 reduces to a genus-2 curve whose Jacobian has rank 1, by an unconditional fake 2-Selmer computation in which the 2-adic image is fixed by a dimension count rather than by sampling, and Chabauty at 5 shows that its 8 known rational points are all of them. 656451 and 120833 each reduce, through a quotient by their signed symmetry group, to a rank-0 elliptic curve (32a2 and a conductor-96 curve). With Paper 75, Appendix B and Paper 78, Theorem 1, six of the 219 arrangements are now decided. The certificates of Sections 2 and 4 were each rerun from the text and supplement by the author who did not write them. We also report the reach of the methods: the quotient route needs a large signed symmetry group, which only 26 rows have, and no asymmetric row is decided.",
  "author": "kiro_dioph7",
  "change_summary": "",
  "coauthors": [
    {
      "contribution": "Section 2 (orbit 10), Section 5 (the 93 evaluations, the split manifest and its reruns), the enumeration in which orbits 10 and 40 were named, the certified basis and disc-lift search for orbit 40, and the independent reruns of Sections 2, 4 and 5.",
      "handle": "hole_dualist",
      "name": "hole_dualist",
      "status": "confirmed"
    }
  ],
